[
  {
    "question_id": "math-86e3a0e79ac66175",
    "question": "Maths 3\nInverse Operations",
    "questiontopic": "Unclassified",
    "answer": "",
    "explanation": ""
  },
  {
    "question_id": "math-63164a4d5bb4a960",
    "question": "Inverse means the opposite in effect or the reverse of.",
    "questiontopic": "Unclassified",
    "answer": "",
    "explanation": ""
  },
  {
    "question_id": "math-1d68fb9b69b4e3e7",
    "question": "The Inverse of Adding is Adding moves you one way, subtracting\nSubtracting moves you the opposite way.\nExamples:\n20 + 9 = 29 can be reversed by 29 - 9 = 20\nYou end up back\n15 - 3 = 12 can be reversed by 12 +3 = 15 Sabisneyaint HUE\nThe Inverse of Multiplying is Multiplying can be \"undone\" by\nDividing dividing.\nExamples:",
    "questiontopic": "Unclassified",
    "answer": "",
    "explanation": ""
  },
  {
    "question_id": "math-28512b79008e8b0b",
    "question": "5 x 9 = 45 can be reversed by 45 +9 =5",
    "questiontopic": "Unclassified",
    "answer": "",
    "explanation": ""
  },
  {
    "question_id": "math-80ee45d0d75c0697",
    "question": "It works the other way around too, dividing can be undone my multiplying.\n10 + 2 =5 can be reversed by 5 x 2= 10",
    "questiontopic": "Unclassified",
    "answer": "",
    "explanation": ""
  },
  {
    "question_id": "math-74d4fe9983c06b9c",
    "question": "But Not With 0. You can't divide by 0, so don't try!",
    "questiontopic": "Unclassified",
    "answer": "",
    "explanation": ""
  },
  {
    "question_id": "math-80d7a1dea930212f",
    "question": "Example: 5 x 0 = 0 cannot be reversed by 0 + 0 = ???",
    "questiontopic": "Unclassified",
    "answer": "",
    "explanation": ""
  },
  {
    "question_id": "math-aca9c644665d7644",
    "question": "Using Brackets In Calculations",
    "questiontopic": "Unclassified",
    "answer": "",
    "explanation": ""
  },
  {
    "question_id": "math-0b09da8eb7e76798",
    "question": "When you see calculations inside brackets you do them first.",
    "questiontopic": "Unclassified",
    "answer": "",
    "explanation": ""
  },
  {
    "question_id": "math-e6b5398dbf11a104",
    "question": "Example: (3 + 2) x (6-4) | The brackets group 8 + 2\n= (5) x (2) together, and 6 — 4 together,\n- 10 so they are calculated first.",
    "questiontopic": "Unclassified",
    "answer": "",
    "explanation": ""
  },
  {
    "question_id": "math-50ef2367a3902357",
    "question": "When you have more complicated calculations, it is a good idea to use different\ntypes of brackets to separate the various groups of calculations:",
    "questiontopic": "Unclassified",
    "answer": "",
    "explanation": ""
  },
  {
    "question_id": "math-65da5e599dbf5d32",
    "question": "Example: [(3 +2) x (6-4) +2] x4 | The () brackets group 3 + 2",
    "questiontopic": "Unclassified",
    "answer": "",
    "explanation": ""
  },
  {
    "question_id": "math-9763af2a54f5ca20",
    "question": "= [(5) x (2) +2] x 4 together, and 6 — 4 together,\n= [10+ 2] x4 and the [ ] brackets tell you to\n= [12] x 4 do all the calculations inside",
    "questiontopic": "Unclassified",
    "answer": "",
    "explanation": ""
  },
  {
    "question_id": "math-c55b1f0bc6e9ba2a",
    "question": "= 48 them before multiplying by 4.",
    "questiontopic": "Unclassified",
    "answer": "",
    "explanation": ""
  },
