  {
    "question_id": "math-2cd65d348e673dc4",
    "question": "21. OW2+n)= 22. 10(7 +f) =\n\nIntroduction to Equations\n\nAn equation is a number sentence that says two things are equal (i.e. the\nsame). It always has an equals sign. What is on one side of the equals sign is\nworth the same as what is on the other side.\n\nExamples:\n2a=6 means 2a is worth the same as 6\n2y+3=4y-1 means 2y + 3 is worth the same as 4y - 1\n\nWhen there is only one unknown in an equation it is possible to solve the\nequation.\n\nSolving the equation means finding out what number the letter stands for. It is\nlike cracking a code.\n\nPage 4 of 10",
    "questiontopic": "Unclassified",
    "answer": "",
    "explanation": ""
  },
  {
    "question_id": "math-b3b0e5f1e42acf67",
    "question": "44. What is the number?\n\nThis can be written as the equation 8n + 4 = 44. The equation can be solved to\n] find the number, n.\n\nThere are two main ways that an equation can be solved: using ideas of\nbalancing and using inverses. Both ways are shown below:\n\nUsing Balancing\nWe know that both sides of an equation are worth\nthe same — in other words they balance.\n8n+4=44\n\n8n+4 44\n\n—)\n\nBalancing Rules:\n\ne Whatever you do to one side of an equation, do it to the other side\nalso so that both sides still balance. So you can add, subtract, multiply\nor divide one side of an equation - but you must do the same to the other\nside too!\n\ne Aim to get the letter on its own on one side of the equation and the\nnumbers on the other side.\n\ne To get rid of something from one side, do the opposite or inverse. If it's an\nadd then subtract, if it's a times then divide and vice versa.\n\nPage 5 of 10",
    "questiontopic": "Unclassified",
    "answer": "",
    "explanation": ""
  },
  {
    "question_id": "math-a51a4d192ee1e1f7",
    "question": "Maths 3",
    "questiontopic": "Unclassified",
    "answer": "",
    "explanation": ""
  },
  {
    "question_id": "math-c90cf277760ea567",
    "question": "8n + 4 = 44 (a) Get rid of the + 4 on the left.\n- 4 -4 Do the opposite - subtract 4 from both sides.\n8n = 40 (b) 8n means 8 x n. Get rid of the 8 x.\n+8 +8 Do the opposite - divide by 8 on both sides\nn=5",
    "questiontopic": "Unclassified",
    "answer": "",
    "explanation": ""
  },
  {
    "question_id": "math-97afd64092c97292",
    "question": "Keep imagining a weighing scale and always ask: What can I do to take weight\noff from one side so that only the variable is left. Examine the equation and\ndecide how you can add/subtract/multiply/divide both sides so that you are left\nwith only the variable on one side.",
    "questiontopic": "Unclassified",
    "answer": "",
    "explanation": ""
  },
  {
    "question_id": "math-2c004e2cf113d247",
    "question": "Remember:",
    "questiontopic": "Unclassified",
    "answer": "",
    "explanation": ""
  },
  {
    "question_id": "math-71b6247da8043527",
    "question": "e Adding and subtracting are the inverse (or opposite) of each other.\ne Multiplying and dividing are the inverse of each other.",
    "questiontopic": "Unclassified",
    "answer": "",
    "explanation": ""
  },
  {
    "question_id": "math-5de671036649ab84",
    "question": "e Ifthe same quantity is added to or subtracted from both sides of an\nequation, the two sides of the equation remain in balance.",
    "questiontopic": "Unclassified",
    "answer": "",
    "explanation": ""
  },
  {
    "question_id": "math-4ac9c7851228bebb",
    "question": "e Ifboth sides of an equation are multiplied or divided by the same number,\nthe two sides of the equation remain in balance.",
    "questiontopic": "Unclassified",
    "answer": "",
    "explanation": ""
  },
  {
    "question_id": "math-a09008e9c87604e7",
    "question": "More Examples:",
    "questiontopic": "Unclassified",
    "answer": "",
